Abstract = {The Hha and TomB proteins from Escherichia coli form an oxygen-dependent
   toxin-antitoxin (TA) system. Here we show that YmoB, the Yersinia
   orthologue of TomB, and its single cysteine variant {[}C117S] YmoB can
   replace TomB as antitoxins in E. coli. In contrast to other TA systems,
   {[}C117S] YmoB transiently interacts with Hha (rather than forming a
   stable complex) and enhances the spontaneous oxidation of the Hha
   conserved cysteine residue to a -SO(x)Hcontaining species (sulfenic,
   sulfinic or sulfonic acid), which destabilizes the toxin. The nuclear
   magnetic resonance structure of {[}C117S]YmoB and the homology model of
   TomB show that the two proteins form a four-helix bundle with a
   conserved buried cysteine connected to the exterior by a channel with a
   diameter comparable to that of an oxygen molecule. The Hha interaction
   site is located on the opposite side of the helix bundle.},
