Month = {JAN 2},
Abstract = {Linking clinical multi-omics with mechanistic studies may improve the
   understanding of rare cancers. We leverage two precision oncology
   programs to investigate rhabdomyosarcoma with FUS/EWSR1-TFCP2 fusions,
   an orphan malignancy without effective therapies. All tumors exhibit
   outlier ALK expression, partly accompanied by intragenic deletions and
   aberrant splicing resulting in ALK variants that are oncogenic and
   sensitive to ALK inhibitors. Additionally, recurrent CKDN2A/MTAP
   co-deletions provide a rationale for PRMT5-targeted therapies.
   Functional studies show that FUS-TFCP2 blocks myogenic differentiation,
   induces transcription of ALK and truncated TERT, and inhibits DNA
   repair. Unlike other fusion-driven sarcomas, TFCP2-rearranged tumors
   exhibit genomic instability and signs of defective homologous
   recombination. DNA methylation profiling demonstrates a close
   relationship with undifferentiated sarcomas. In two patients, sarcoma
   was preceded by benign lesions carrying FUS-TFCP2, indicating stepwise
   sarcomagenesis. This study illustrates the potential of linking
   precision oncology with preclinical research to gain insight into the
   classification, pathogenesis, and therapeutic vulnerabilities of rare
   cancers.},
